================================================================================
TEST T33246 -- IPv4_ARP - ARP Polling
================================================================================
Run:      2026-10-15 19:29-20:00 NZDT (the DUT's clock reads UTC and runs 3 s behind
          the box's, measured by bracketing `show clock` between two box readings, so
          the DUT log timestamps quoted below are directly comparable with the box's
          capture times. Device times below are swi_d's own UTC.)
Bench:    tb277, consoles u2 = swi_d (DUT); Test Engineer bidhanc@bidhanc-dl
Tester:   bench-runner
Graded:   tester
Case:     T33246 "IPv4_ARP - ARP Polling". No authored step text exists for this case,
          in AWPTCM or in ck.db: an exact-title lookup found step text for one other
          case in this campaign and none for this one, so there are no authored steps
          and no authored expected results. The headings below say what was actually
          exercised. The case was graded against:
            - X960 platform support TPS row 18 "ARP Polling", priority 1, "Yes", note
              column empty. That row is Project 2561, "ARP-polling for Web Auth": on
              this platform "ARP Polling" is NOT a generic ARP-cache refresh feature but
              web-authentication polling -- the device sends DIRECTED ARP requests to an
              already web-authenticated supplicant and deauthenticates it after
              `failcount` unanswered requests. There is no `arp polling` anywhere under
              `arp ?`;
            - the Project 2561 functional spec. Its proposed syntax `auth-web-server
              [arp-poll | ping-poll] enable` does NOT exist on this build, re-confirmed
              here by read-only probe (STEP 0); the shipped syntax is `auth-web-server
              ping-poll type arp`, and the spec's revision 0.3 is logged as "Change
              configuration syntax", so the shipped CLI is the intended one. That is a
              DOCUMENTATION DIVERGENCE, not a product defect, and the case is not graded
              UNSUPPORTED for it -- but anyone grading T33246 against the spec text
              verbatim would wrongly call the feature absent. The IP Feature Overview
              and Configuration Guide contains no mention of polling at all;
            - the DUT's own CLI surface, read off by a read-only `?` probe (STEP 0).
          Objective: with a web-authenticated supplicant present, `auth-web-server
          ping-poll type arp` + `enable` makes the DUT send DIRECTED (unicast) ARP
          requests to that supplicant at `interval`; `type ping` sends ICMP instead; a
          supplicant that stops answering is deauthenticated after `failcount`
          unanswered polls; the configuration is removable and survives a reboot.
          6 steps as executed, plus a baseline.
Build:    swi_d  sin_x96056-20261012-24  (flash:/x960-tb277.rel, build date Mon Oct 12 2026)
          AT-x960-56YSQ, S/N A2WQS700B, standalone, console u2 @115200
Configs:  swi_d.cfg (saved to startup: no). PROVENANCE: this file is NOT an in-run
          capture. The capture point before step 1 was missed, so the file was produced
          afterwards by RE-APPLYING the identical setup command set, capturing
          `show running-config`, and tearing down again. The configuration it holds is
          the command set the case ran under; the only content that cannot match is the
          RADIUS key hash, which the DUT re-encrypts per entry. The file's own header
          line says the same.

VERDICT: PASS -- "ARP Polling" on this platform is web-auth ping-polling, and it works
as the functional spec requires: the poll is DIRECTED to the supplicant, it runs at the
configured interval, the type selects ARP or ICMP, and an unanswered supplicant is
deauthenticated after exactly `failcount` polls.
      baseline, polling disabled .......... 40 s with an authenticated supplicant
                                            present: 0 frames captured, of any kind
      `ping-poll type arp` + `enable` ..... 5 polls in 45 s at `interval 10`
      DIRECTED, not broadcast ............. every poll's Ethernet dst is
                                            00:0d:b9:55:77:0e, the supplicant's own MAC
      poll content ........................ who-has 10.38.22.33 tell 10.38.22.34, from
                                            the DUT's SVI MAC; all 5 answered
      `type ping` control ................. ICMP echo requests at the same cadence and
                                            ZERO DUT-sourced ARP
      deauthentication .................... exactly 3 directed polls (failcount 3), 0
                                            replies, then polling STOPS
      named by the DUT's own log .......... "Supplicant ckpoll logoff, Mac
                                            000d.b955.770e", then "unauthorized"
      re-authentication ................... supplicant authorizes again and polling
                                            resumes, 4 polls in 35 s, all answered
      removal ............................. `enable` line gone, parameters kept, and
                                            0 frames in 40 s
      reboot .............................. all four settings and the directed-poll
                                            behaviour survive
CAVEAT:  Proven with ONE supplicant on one port, `interval 10`, `timeout 1`,
         `failcount 3`, over HTTP. The default interval (30) and failcount (5) were read
         from the running state but not timed. `reauth-timer-refresh` was not exercised.
         The box's eth3 segment is NOT point-to-point: a third station (10.38.22.41,
         MAC 00:0c:25:2a:a5:53) is live on it and appears in some captures below, so
         every count here is matched on BOTH Ethernet MACs and the deauthentication is
         attributed by the DUT's own by-MAC log line rather than by a supplicant count,
         which on a shared segment could move for an unrelated reason.

