STEP 1 -- `ping-poll type arp` + `enable`: DIRECTED ARP requests at the configured interval  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
awplus# configure terminal
awplus(config)# auth-web-server ping-poll type arp
awplus(config)# auth-web-server ping-poll interval 10
awplus(config)# auth-web-server ping-poll failcount 3
awplus(config)# auth-web-server ping-poll enable
awplus(config)# end
awplus# show auth-web-server
  PingPolling: enabled
  PingPollingType: ARP
  PingInterval: 10
  Timeout: 1
  FailCount: 3

  sudo python3.8 tools/arpleg.py --tag L1-arp-poll --out <capture dir> \
    --iface eth3 --sniff-only --wait 45
cap L1-arp-poll-eth3.pcap: 10 frame(s)
  x5  ARP-REQ   who-has 10.38.22.33 tell 10.38.22.34
                                     [eth e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e]
                                     <-- Ethernet dst is the SUPPLICANT'S MAC, not
                                         ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff
  x5  ARP-REPLY 10.38.22.33 is-at 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e
                                     [eth 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e -> e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43]

  Three separate requirements met in one capture:
    - COUNT AND CADENCE: 5 polls in 45 s with `interval 10`. The rate matches the
      configured interval, so the frames are the poll and not something incidental.
    - DIRECTED, NOT BROADCAST: the Ethernet destination of every poll is
      00:0d:b9:55:77:0e, the supplicant's own MAC. This is the spec's distinguishing
      requirement, and it is what separates a poll from an ordinary ARP resolution,
      which this DUT broadcasts.
    - Sender is the DUT's vlan10 address 10.38.22.34 and its SVI MAC; the target is the
      supplicant's address, and the supplicant answered all 5.

  => STEP 1 PASS: enabling ARP polling takes the segment from zero frames in 40 s to 5
     directed, answered polls in 45 s at exactly the configured interval, addressed
     unicast to the authenticated supplicant -- the behaviour the feature specifies.

STEP 2 -- control: `type ping` must produce ICMP, not ARP                  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: same supplicant, same interval, one word of configuration changed. This is
  what makes STEP 1's ARP frames attributable to the `type arp` setting rather than to
  polling in general.

awplus(config)# auth-web-server ping-poll type ping
awplus# show auth-web-server -> PingPolling: enabled / PingPollingType: Ping / PingInterval: 10

  leg `L2-icmp-poll` (--sniff-only --wait 45):
cap L2-icmp-poll-eth3.pcap: 12 frame(s)
  x4  ICMP t8/c0 10.38.22.34 -> 10.38.22.33  [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e]
  x4  ICMP t0/c0 10.38.22.33 -> 10.38.22.34  [00:0d:b9:55:77:0e -> e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43]
  x2  ARP-REQ   who-has 10.38.22.34 tell 10.38.22.33 [00:0d:b9:55:77:0e -> e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43]
  x2  ARP-REPLY 10.38.22.34 is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43  [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e]

  The DUT now emits ICMP echo requests (type 8) at the same cadence and ZERO ARP
  requests of its own. The two ARP requests present go in the OPPOSITE direction --
  source 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e, the box refreshing its own cache for the DUT -- so they are
  not polls.

  => STEP 2 PASS: `type arp` and `type ping` are distinguishable on the wire, each
     producing only its own protocol. The ARP frames counted in STEP 1 are therefore
     attributable to the type setting, not merely to polling being on.

STEP 3 -- deauthentication after `failcount` unanswered polls              PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: the supplicant must stop answering its directed ARP polls while the LINK
  STAYS UP -- a link-down would deauthenticate it for an unrelated reason and prove
  nothing. That was done with a host-NIC attribute, declared and reverted:

    sudo ip link set eth3 arp off     # eth3 then reads NOARP; link, carrier and the
                                      # DUT's port1.0.10 all stay up
    ... measurement ...
    sudo ip link set eth3 arp on      # reverted; NOARP flag count back to 0, verified

awplus(config)# auth-web-server ping-poll type arp
awplus# show auth-web-server   -> PingPollingType: ARP, FailCount: 3
awplus# show auth supplicant   -> Supplicant name: ckpoll, portStatus: Authorized

  leg `L3-deauth` (--sniff-only --wait 70):
cap L3-deauth-eth3.pcap: 7 frame(s)
  x3  ARP-REQ who-has 10.38.22.33 tell 10.38.22.34  [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e]
  x3  ARP-REQ who-has 10.38.22.41 tell 10.38.22.41  [00:0c:25:2a:a5:53 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
  x1  IP p2   0.0.0.0 -> 224.0.0.22                 [00:0c:25:2a:a5:53 -> 01:00:5e:00:00:16]

  Reading, and this is the limb that must not rest on a supplicant count, so it does not:
    - The DUT sent EXACTLY 3 directed ARP requests to 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e. `failcount` is 3.
    - NOT ONE ARP-REPLY from 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e appears -- against 5 replies for 5 polls
      in STEP 1. The polls genuinely went unanswered.
    - Polling then STOPPED: no 4th poll in the remaining ~40 s of the 70 s window. The
      DUT gave up at failcount rather than continuing.
    - The other 4 frames carry 00:0c:25:2a:a5:53, the third station on the segment --
      a gratuitous ARP for its own address and an IGMP report. Different MAC, different
      addresses, listed as separate flows; neither is a poll and neither is answered by
      anything.

  The DUT's own log names the supplicant and the event, by MAC:
2026 Oct 15 06:35:41 802.1X[1061]: Web Authentication successful for
   ckpoll@000d.b955.770e(10.38.22.33) on port1.0.10
2026 Oct 15 06:39:55 802.1X[1061]: port1.0.10: Supplicant ckpoll logoff, Mac 000d.b955.770e
2026 Oct 15 06:39:56 802.1X[1061]: port1.0.10: Supplicant ckpoll unauthorized,
   Mac 000d.b955.770e

awplus# show auth supplicant      -> authorizedSupplicantNum: 0, Supplicant name: -
awplus# show auth interface port1.0.10 -> portStatus: Unauthorized

  The timing closes it: the NOARP window opened at about 06:38:45; three polls at
  `interval 10` with `timeout 1` ran out at about 06:39:55; the logoff is stamped
  06:39:55 with the unauthorized line one second later.

  => STEP 3 PASS: the DUT sends exactly `failcount` directed polls, receives no answer
     to any of them, stops polling, and deauthenticates that supplicant -- naming it and
     its MAC in its own log. The verdict rests on the three unanswered DIRECTED polls
     plus the by-MAC log line, not on a supplicant count, which on this shared segment
     would have been ambiguous.

