  leg `L4-resumed` (--sniff-only --wait 35):
cap L4-resumed-eth3.pcap: 11 frame(s)
  x4  ARP-REQ   who-has 10.38.22.33 tell 10.38.22.34 [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e]
  x4  ARP-REPLY 10.38.22.33 is-at 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e  [00:0d:b9:55:77:0e -> e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43]
  x1  ARP-REQ   who-has 10.38.22.34 tell 10.38.22.34 [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
  x1  ARP-REQ   who-has 10.38.22.33 tell 10.38.22.41 [00:0c:25:2a:a5:53 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
  x1  ARP-REPLY 10.38.22.33 is-at 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e  [00:0d:b9:55:77:0e -> 00:0c:25:2a:a5:53]

  4 directed polls in 35 s, all answered. The single broadcast ARP from the DUT for its
  OWN address (10.38.22.34 tell 10.38.22.34) is a gratuitous ARP / duplicate-address
  check, not a poll; the last two frames are the third station again, separable by MAC.

  => STEP 4 PASS: the deauthentication was a poll timeout, not a permanent state. The
     supplicant re-authenticated normally and polling resumed at the same cadence and
     with the same directed addressing, so STEP 3 removed the authorisation without
     disabling the mechanism.

